      <h1 class="narrative-title">Lower Duwamish Waterway Superfund Cleanup</h1>
      <p class="narrative-lede">
        Understanding Seattle's industrial river corridor: EPA Region 10 cleanup progress, sediment dredging, industrial soil source controls, resident fish advisories, and municipal drinking water safety.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Real Estate Coach Callout -->
    <div class="coach-callout">
      <h3>💡 Real Estate Coach's Take</h3>
      <p>
        The Lower Duwamish Waterway is the industrial heart of Seattle's maritime history, running through South Park, Georgetown, and Tukwila. First, a essential piece of clarity: <strong>the Duwamish Superfund site has zero effect on home drinking tap water</strong>. Municipal water across Seattle, King County, and Snohomish County is sourced from protected, high-elevation mountain watersheds (the Cedar and Tolt rivers). The Superfund designation focuses strictly on heavy industrial riverbed sediments, historical storm runoff, and riverbanks. Multi-million-dollar EPA dredging and soil source controls have significantly improved the corridor, turning sites like Terminal 117 into pristine public parks (Duwamish River People's Park).
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Educational Body Section -->
    <section class="narrative-section">
      <h2>The 5.5-Mile Industrial River Cleanup</h2>
      <p>
        In 2001, the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) listed a 5.5-mile stretch of the Lower Duwamish Waterway as a national Superfund cleanup site due to over a century of industrial activity along its banks:
      </p>

      <table class="comparison-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Cleanup Target Area</th>
            <th>Primary Contaminants</th>
            <th>EPA Remediation Strategy</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Riverbed Sediments</strong></td>
            <td>PCBs, dioxins, furans, arsenic, polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs).</td>
            <td>Dredging heavy toxic spots, capping riverbed sections with clean sand/gravel, and monitored natural recovery.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Industrial Drainage Basins</strong></td>
            <td>Stormwater runoff and pipe discharges from manufacturing sites.</td>
            <td>WA Dept of Ecology source control to prevent re-contamination of riverbed.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Public Parks & Riverbanks</strong></td>
            <td>Legacy shoreline topsoil contamination.</td>
            <td>Complete excavation of impacted soil and replanting with native estuary vegetation.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Resident Fish & Shellfish Health Advisories</h2>
      <p>
        While migratory salmon pass through the river briefly on their way to spawning grounds and remain safe to eat, resident sea life lives in the river year-round:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Resident Fish & Crab Advisory:</strong> The WA Department of Health recommends avoiding consumption of resident fish (such as perch, flounder, and sole), crabs, and shellfish harvested directly from the Lower Duwamish River.</li>
        <li><strong>Migratory Salmon Safety:</strong> Coho, Chinook, Pink, and Chum salmon spend minimal time in riverbed sediments, making them safe for recreational fishing under standard state seasons.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Living Near the Duwamish River Corridor</h2>
      <p>
        For homebuyers evaluating properties in Georgetown, South Park, Tukwila, or Beacon Hill, the river cleanup represents a major long-term public investment. Active community advocacy groups like the Duwamish River Community Coalition (DRCC) work directly with the EPA and Port of Seattle to ensure continuous air and soil monitoring throughout South Seattle.
      </p>
    </section>

